"""Additional explanations and structured visual content for the A4 reference edition."""

COMPANY_EXTRA = [
('How the product fits institutional work', 'A typical enquiry begins with a limited reference, such as a name, account, phone or vehicle. Mosaic provides a route from that reference to related records and the source material supporting each association. The officer can compare different views of the same case, develop a lead, seek review and prepare a report without losing the relationship between the original record and the resulting interpretation. This continuity is the central product proposition: analytical tools and accountable case work are treated as parts of the same process.'),
('The information that must remain connected', 'An effective working record needs more than a consolidated value. It needs the source from which that value was obtained, its date, the transformation applied to it and the reason it was associated with another record. These details allow an officer to distinguish an exact identifier match from a similar name, a recorded observation from a registered address, or a transaction from an inferred relationship. Preserving those distinctions makes later review more informative and reduces the need to reconstruct the analysis from separate documents.'),
('A shared model across tools', 'The analytical views work from a common representation of records and relationships. Selecting an account in a dossier can lead to its transactions, a financial trace or the wider case network. Selecting a place can lead to a geographic enquiry and then to the records behind an observation. This shared model allows the officer to change the question being asked without treating each view as a separate collection of facts. Source references remain important when the same object is represented across different analytical perspectives.'),
('Continuity between stages', 'The workflow is iterative. A report review may reveal a missing source, an identity comparison may require a revised intake, or a newly available transaction may change the next line of enquiry. Mosaic retains the case context as work moves back to an earlier stage. The team can examine the current records, identify the decision already taken and record the next action. A closed loop of preparation, examination and review is more useful than treating an investigation as a sequence that can only move forwards.'),
('Responsibility follows the action', 'The relevant distinction is the decision being performed. Preparing an extract, approving its publication, examining a case and auditing access are different responsibilities even when staff work within one institution. Mosaic applies the relevant checks at those decision points. The team can therefore review who proposed an action, who decided it and what reason was recorded, rather than relying only on the user’s job title or the appearance of a screen.'),
('Reviewing a disputed result', 'When a result is questioned, the reviewer can move back through its supporting records, identity associations and preparation context. A conflicting value should be examined against its source and date rather than treated as a formatting problem. Likewise, an intact file fingerprint answers a question about bytes, while the custody record answers questions about handling. Maintaining these separate references helps the institution identify whether a concern relates to the original material, its interpretation or an action taken within the application.'),
('Preparation for operational use', 'Implementation planning should identify who supplies each extract, how changes are received, who reviews proposed publication and which role maintains source-quality issues. It should also establish how identity is provisioned, where exports may be released, which geographic services are available and who operates backup and recovery. These arrangements connect the product’s implemented capabilities to the institution’s actual working process. They must be established for the deployment rather than inferred from the demonstration configuration.'),
('Interpreting the examples', 'A figure illustrates a particular state of the product. A filter, selection or available action may differ with the acting role and the records loaded into the case. Read the screenshot together with its explanation and the chapter introduction. Where a procedure spans several screens, follow the figures in order to distinguish setup, review and completion. The editable source preserves the original screenshots separately from the written explanations, making both the visual material and its interpretation available for review.'),
]

CHAPTER_EXTRA = {
'01': ('Practical sequence', 'Begin by confirming the identity and active case, then review outstanding decisions before opening a new enquiry. Use search when a reference is already known and the work queue when the task arises from an update or request. After following an item, return to the case context to determine whether the next step is further examination, an approval request or preparation of a report. A refusal should be understood as an access decision rather than a failed search.'),
'02': ('Working across the case lifecycle', 'A useful case narrative connects the initial reference, the records examined and the decisions taken. The board provides orientation; the network and timeline support examination; the lead board records progress. Exhibits and reports preserve selected material for review and release. These functions should be used together so that a report conclusion can be traced to its supporting records and the case status reflects the actual stage of work. Closing the case records an outcome while leaving retention to its separate controls.'),
'03': ('Evaluating the finding', 'A lead should be examined against the detector’s stated question. Identify the records that meet the condition, check their dates and compare the explanation with other plausible accounts of the activity. Where the available material supports further work, the case request should state what is to be investigated and its recorded basis. Where it does not, the dismissal reason should explain the disposition sufficiently for another reviewer to understand why the lead was not progressed.'),
'04': ('From alert to case analysis', 'Treat the alert as a reference to a set of transactions and relationships. Establish which accounts are involved, how the amounts were calculated and whether the relevant dates support the proposed sequence. The dossier supplies entity context; tracing examines direction and endpoints; the network shows the wider structure. The resulting case narrative should identify which records support the interpretation and which aspects remain unresolved, rather than repeating the rule name as a conclusion.'),
'05': ('Maintaining an active enquiry', 'A watch is most useful when its recorded reason explains what the case expects to learn. Review the available updates, follow the supporting records and mark items considered so the team can distinguish fresh material. When the enquiry no longer requires monitoring, stop the watch with a reason. For a saved query, review both the search criteria and newly matching records; a match can reflect a new ingestion or changed visibility rather than a newly occurring event.'),
'06': ('A disciplined analytical sequence', 'Start with the question, then choose the layout and relationship types that address it. Inspect a small number of relevant records before relying on a structural measure. When a path or community appears significant, examine its component links and source references. Keep the selected scope and loaded graph in mind when comparing results. A visually central record may represent a shared service or a commonly occurring identifier rather than the operational centre of an activity.'),
'07': ('Reading across tabs', 'The dossier is most useful when its tabs are read together. An apparent financial change can be compared with the timeline and relevant source rows. A communication contact can be examined in the network and through the identifier associated with it. A travel booking can be checked against its date and supporting reference. This approach preserves the distinction between the presence of a record and the interpretation placed upon it, while reducing repeated searches for the same entity.'),
'08': ('Examining a route', 'Choose the direction and level of detail before interpreting the shape of a trace. Party aggregation can make a broad route readable, while account detail exposes separate instruments held by one party. Expand or narrow the query to answer a specific question, then inspect the relevant transaction references. Where a route reaches a boundary, determine whether it reflects the selected hop limit, the minimum amount or an absence of further records before describing it as an endpoint.'),
'09': ('Assessing relationship meaning', 'Read a chain as a sequence of individually supported statements. An ownership link, a call and a shared address are different forms of association and should not be described interchangeably. Consider the time and source context of each step, particularly when the chain combines records from different periods. The result can identify useful questions for follow-up, but the significance of the full chain depends on the evidence for its individual connections.'),
'10': ('From location to evidence', 'Use the overview to identify the relevant place, then define an area enquiry with an explicit period and radius. Compare observations by source and time, and open the associated records to understand what was observed. A camera read, a tower record and a travel booking have different geographic meanings. Retain those distinctions when selecting material for a report or deciding to watch the area for further available activity.'),
'11': ('Developing a repeatable query', 'Begin with the reference or category that is known, then narrow the result set using the available filters. Check a sample of the underlying records before interpreting an aggregate distribution. When a query is useful for continued case work, save it with a reason that explains the investigative question. Subsequent results can then be assessed against that question rather than treated as an unstructured list of additional records.'),
'12': ('Reviewing the effect of an intake', 'A reviewer should be able to move from the proposed operation back to the prepared row and original source. Examine matching evidence and conflicting values before approving the publication. The legal order and explanatory note establish the submitted context; they should be considered with the actual operations. After publication, inspect the resulting records and assess whether a correction or recorded revert is necessary before relying on newly available information in an analytical conclusion.'),
'13': ('Reasoned identity decisions', 'The quality of a match decision depends on the discriminating information available. A shared name may be weak evidence, while an agreement in a specific identifier can carry a different meaning. Conflicting birth or family details may require further source examination. Record the basis of the decision in terms of those fields and their sources, so that a later reviewer can understand the assessment and reconsider it if corrected information becomes available.'),
'14': ('Evidence-led comparison', 'The workflow should preserve a clear connection between the original still, the selected face and the candidate under review. Check the source photograph and contextual records alongside the visual similarity. The independent reviewer records what supports or contradicts the proposed association. This documented reasoning is more informative than a bare confirmation because it distinguishes the comparison generated by the system from the evidence considered by the person making the decision.'),
'15': ('Operational reading sequence', 'Check the alert’s status and validity period before interpreting its map location. Examine how the affected area was derived and whether any alerts remain unmapped. Use the available district, hospital and gauge references to inform the recorded response. After entering a dispatch, review its resource, quantity and destination in the log. The institution’s arrangements for communicating, delivering and confirming the physical response remain separate from the entry made in Mosaic.'),
'16': ('A focused oversight enquiry', 'An audit review begins with a question about an actor, record or action. Apply the relevant filters, inspect both allowed and refused events and compare the recorded purpose with the activity. Chain verification helps assess the integrity of the recorded sequence; an independently retained root provides an additional comparison point. Retention review addresses a different question: which cases are eligible for removal, which remain held and what action has actually been completed.'),
'17': ('Supporting accountable maintenance', 'Detector documentation and source-quality records should be used together when investigating an unexpected result. Establish the detector’s scope and assumptions, then examine whether the source material contains the required fields and relationships. A source correction, a configuration change and an implementation change are different remedies. The administration views provide information for that assessment without replacing the case reviewer’s responsibility to evaluate the evidence behind a particular finding.'),
}

# Additional, screen-specific reading guidance. These paragraphs explain how to
# interpret the displayed state and carry its result into the actual workflow.
SCREEN_CONTEXT = {
'01-overview': ('Using the overview', 'The queue and case summary serve different purposes. The queue identifies work awaiting attention, while the case panel supplies the investigation to which that work belongs. Opening an item provides its detail rather than completing it. The officer should follow the relevant request, record or lead and use the action available in that workflow. This preserves a distinction between noticing an item, examining it and recording a decision.'),
'01-search': ('Evaluating a search result', 'A result should be selected using both its identifier and the surrounding context. Two similar names can belong to different people, and several types of object may match the same enquiry. Open the relevant record to inspect its particulars and sources before following a relationship or adding material to the case. A withheld count reports that some matches are restricted; it is not a reason to infer their identities or content. The available result remains tied to the active case.'),
'01-search-script': ('Identity remains a separate question', 'The spelling variants improve retrieval across sources that record names differently. They do not replace examination of a person’s other details. Compare the family information, birth details, district and identifiers available in the record, and use the identity-review workflow where the association remains uncertain. A name found through transliteration can be relevant to the enquiry even when the original source preserves a different script. Keeping that original representation helps a reviewer understand how the source described the person.'),
'01-open-case': ('Completing the case context', 'The title should identify the work clearly, while the purpose and recorded basis explain why the case is being opened. Review those fields together before submitting the form. A reference such as an FIR can connect existing material to the case, but the officer should still examine what has been associated and whether it is relevant. After creation, confirm the selected case in the workspace before searching personal records. Later reviewers can then relate access and export events to the stated context.'),
'02-overview-crime': ('Acting on an update', 'The time shown on an update is part of its context. Newly available material may describe an earlier event, so the officer should open the source record before interpreting it as recent activity. Category filters help separate approval-related work from leads and observations. Where an update changes a line of enquiry, continue into the case workflow and record the appropriate follow-up rather than treating the queue’s priority as an investigative conclusion.'),
'02-overview-supervisor': ('Reviewing the request', 'The supervisor should consider what is requested, the case to which it belongs and the basis provided by the proposing officer. Approval and refusal are decisions on that request, not general changes to the officer’s permissions. A useful reason explains the decision in terms of the requested work and available material. The resulting status allows the requesting officer to continue or revise the work with a clear understanding of the recorded outcome.'),
'01-light-theme': ('Presentation and operating context', 'Theme selection is an interface preference and should not be confused with a different user role. The same case remains selected, and available controls continue to reflect the acting account. Before demonstrating an approval or audit workflow, check the identity in the account menu rather than inferring it from the appearance of the screen. The light and dark views are useful alternatives for reading the same information under different working conditions; they do not alter the meaning of its handling marks.'),
'01-account-menu': ('Interpreting the demonstration', 'The picker allows the product demonstration to show how the same case appears to different roles. It should be used to understand the separation between preparation, supervision and oversight. Changing the demonstration identity changes the actor for subsequent requests. When documenting or reviewing an action, read the acting identity and case together so that the available control is understood in the correct role context.'),
'01-role-refused': ('Understanding the outcome', 'A refusal establishes that the selected identity cannot use the requested work area. It should not be interpreted as evidence that the relevant records do not exist. Returning to the overview restores a route to the functions available to that role. If an operational task requires a different authorisation, it must follow the institution’s configured process; changing a URL or choosing another visual layout does not create that authority.'),
'03-case-board': ('Reading the summary', 'Counts and totals orient the team to the loaded case material. They should be read alongside the supporting records and current case scope, particularly when the board presents an analytical priority or a financial endpoint. The board is the starting point for detailed examination rather than a substitute for the underlying transaction, relationship or exhibit.'),
'03-case-suspect-open': ('From priority to enquiry', 'Read the explanation account by account and relationship by relationship. A large transaction amount, rapid onward movement or association with another record can justify further examination, but each fact has its own source and time context. Use the available analytical links to verify the relationship and identify what additional information would clarify it. If the matter becomes a case lead, describe the question to be investigated rather than converting the displayed priority into an established conclusion.'),
'03-case-leads-board': ('Interpreting board states', 'The column identifies the current stage of work on a lead. Awaiting approval means that a decision is still required; Action authorised records the supervisory step, while Closed indicates that the lead is no longer being progressed in the board. Team members should use these states consistently so that the shared view communicates the work accurately. A card’s movement is part of case coordination and should be considered together with its supporting records and the reason for the next action.'),
'03-case-board-5': ('Building a chronology', 'Compare the event reference and date before connecting two entries in a narrative. A filing date, a travel date and a transfer timestamp can describe different stages of activity. The timeline makes their order visible, while the alert identifies a pattern that may warrant closer examination. Use the underlying records to determine whether the sequence supports the proposed explanation. Where records are missing, state the gap in the case analysis rather than assuming that the displayed sequence is complete.'),
'03-case-close': ('Separating closure from retention', 'The closure outcome describes the investigation’s status. A legal hold addresses whether material must remain retained despite an otherwise applicable retention schedule. These decisions answer different questions and require their own recorded context. The retention panel provides the later view of due, scheduled and held cases, allowing the team to inspect eligibility and completed purge actions separately from the case’s closure statement.'),
'03-case-network': ('Navigating the case graph', 'Choose a layout suited to the enquiry, then inspect a relevant record and its links. Relationship filters can separate financial activity from calls, assets or other connections so that the displayed structure remains understandable. A hidden item is only removed from the working view; it is not deleted from the case. When a structural result appears significant, open the corresponding records before describing its meaning in a lead or report. The case board remains the broader reference for progress and outcomes.'),
'03-case-evidence-form': ('Recording the exhibit accurately', 'Use the description and source fields to make the exhibit distinguishable from other files received in the same investigation. Device identifiers should reflect the available seizure or production record, rather than assumptions made from a filename. Review the file and the recorded order before adding it. The fingerprint subsequently answers whether the stored bytes match the registered version; it does not independently establish the truth of the document or the circumstances under which it was produced.'),
'03-case-evidence': ('Preparing for release', 'Before selecting material for an export, inspect the exhibit description, source context and custody events. Verification supports checking the stored file against its registered fingerprint. A release should then be considered in the context of the intended recipient and clearance. Keeping exhibit registration, custody inspection and release as distinct steps makes it easier to understand which file was held, what handling was recorded and which copy was subsequently provided.'),
'03-case-evidence-exports': ('Checking the release package', 'The package should be understood as a set of related materials: the report communicates the reviewed narrative, exhibits provide the source files, custody records describe handling and the manifest supplies hash references. The draft certificate is a document for the responsible officer’s review, not an automatic determination of admissibility. Confirm that the chosen export format contains the material required for the recipient and that the stated clearance is appropriate to the intended release.'),
'03-case-reports-list': ('Selecting a reporting purpose', 'Choose the template according to the intended use of the document. A case diary, an annexure and an intelligence summary organise information differently, even when they draw from the same case records. Review the initial structure, remove material that is not relevant to the report and add the explanation required by its reader. The template provides a starting point for drafting; it does not replace the officer’s responsibility to assess the content and complete the document for its specific purpose.'),
'03-report-editor': ('Maintaining a traceable narrative', 'Use structured blocks for facts that should retain their link to a record, and use authored text to explain their significance. This distinction helps a reader separate what the source states from the officer’s assessment. Since draft facts refresh, review the assembled report again before export, particularly after new material has been published to the case. The resulting PDF fixes the reviewed version at export, while the editable draft remains part of the working process.'),
'03-report-editor-2': ('Choosing relevant detail', 'The selection controls determine which available portions of a record appear in the report. Include the fields needed to understand the point being made and keep the officer’s note connected to those facts. Reordering blocks changes the reading sequence, while removing a block only changes this draft. The original record remains available to the case, allowing another report to use a different selection for a different reader.'),
'03-report-editor-3': ('Managing a reviewed version', 'Unsaved changes and competing updates matter because a report can combine current facts with authored analysis. Review the title, block sequence and notes before saving. If the editor reports a conflict, reload and inspect the current version before continuing the edits. The subsequent export should follow a review of that assembled content so that the fixed copy corresponds to the version the officer intended to release.'),
'03-report-export': ('Review before producing the copy', 'Confirm the reader’s identity and release level alongside the report content. The exported version may circulate separately from the workspace, so its handling information and source references are important to later review. Check that the narrative distinguishes recorded facts from interpretation and that the selected blocks support the report’s purpose. Subsequent changes to the editable draft do not change an already exported copy. A later release should be treated as another reviewed version with its own release context.'),
'19-report-page-1': ('Reading the exported document', 'The page hierarchy identifies the case and organises its selected facts for the recipient. Read a reported amount or relationship with its supporting reference and the officer’s explanation. The document is a presentation of the reviewed material, while the evidence locker and source records remain the references for deeper examination. This distinction helps the recipient identify what is directly recorded, what has been selected for the report and what interpretation has been added by its author.'),
'19-certificate-page-1': ('Document preparation and review', 'Check the exhibit description, recorded device information and handling details against the material actually received. A generated field can only reflect information present in the system; missing or inaccurate source details must be addressed by the responsible officer. Keep the draft associated with its exhibit and the relevant release package so that a reviewer can compare the document with the underlying records. The completed document and its use depend on the applicable process and the authorised signatory’s review.'),
'04-leads-ncb': ('Interpreting supporting evidence', 'The finding should be assessed using the records that satisfy the detector’s condition. Examine the relevant dates, identifiers and measurements, then compare them with the stated innocent explanations and source limitations. Where the pattern supports a specific question, formulate that question in the case request. Where the evidence does not justify further work, record a dismissal reason. This retains the distinction between a detector identifying a pattern and an officer deciding how the institution should respond.'),
'04-leads-ncb-request': ('A reviewable request', 'A well-formed request explains the work proposed and relates its purpose to the supporting lead. The recorded basis should be specific to that enquiry rather than a generic description of the agency’s mission. The separate supervisor can then consider the requested case context and the material supporting it. After the decision, the officer should use the resulting status to continue the authorised work or address the reason for refusal, rather than treating submission as approval.'),
'04-leads-syndicate': ('Continuing the investigation', 'The authorised case context allows the officer to examine relevant identities and relationships through the ordinary case tools. Review the lead against dossiers, the network and the timeline to determine whether the available facts support a coherent line of enquiry. Additional records may strengthen, qualify or contradict the initial pattern. Preserve that distinction in the case narrative so that later readers can understand how the investigation developed from the original detector finding.'),
'05-alerts': ('Organising analytical attention', 'Rule and severity filters answer which findings are being reviewed, not which people have been established as responsible for an offence. Open an alert to examine its actual measurements and records. The same account can appear in several findings for different reasons, so avoid treating the number of alerts as independent confirmation. Use the selected finding to identify the transactions and relationships requiring examination, then relate the result to the active case and the source material available.'),
'05-alerts-filtered': ('Checking a proposed transfer pattern', 'Follow the transaction references in sequence and distinguish an account’s role in the path from the identity of its holder. A visually circular route requires appropriate direction and chronology before it can support an account of funds returning to an earlier point. Amount thresholds and available source coverage can affect the displayed structure. The officer’s explanation should therefore identify the relevant transactions and dates, together with any limitations that affect the interpretation.'),
'06-watchlist': ('Review and disposition', 'Opening an activity item allows the officer to inspect what changed and whether it matters to the enquiry. Marking it reviewed records that the team has considered it; this does not imply that every activity item requires further action. Where the update contributes to a lead or report, follow the corresponding case workflow. If the original reason for monitoring no longer applies, stop the watch and record why it is no longer required.'),
'06-watchlist-area': ('Interpreting location-based updates', 'Read the observation type and recorded time before linking it to the reason for the watch. A plate read identifies a vehicle observation, while a communication record may describe a device’s association with a source location. Their geographic precision and relationship to a person can differ. Open the supporting record and inspect the available owner or subscriber context before treating the update as evidence of a person’s presence or activity inside the watched area.'),
'06-watch-dialog': ('Defining the enquiry', 'The reason should explain why the selected record or area matters to the case. The history interval determines whether the review begins with previously available activity or only the current starting point. Check these settings before creating the watch so the team can interpret subsequent updates consistently. A watch relies on information that becomes available to Mosaic; it is not an instruction to an external source to collect new data. Later review should consider the source and event date behind each update.'),
'06-watchlist-search': ('Reviewing new matches', 'A saved search preserves the query as well as the case context in which it is used. A newly visible result can reflect an additional extract or another permitted update, rather than an event that has just occurred. Open the matching record and examine its date and provenance before deciding whether it changes the enquiry. The recorded reason helps the team understand why the query remains active and when it should be stopped.'),
'07-network': ('Selecting the right perspective', 'Begin with the relationship type relevant to the enquiry, then use the inspector to check individual records. A visual cluster can arise from a common address, device or service as well as from coordinated activity. Structural analysis can identify where to look, but the supporting relationship and its source determine what the connection means. Keep the loaded scope in view when comparing apparent groups or routes.'),
'07-network-list': ('Selection without visual ambiguity', 'The list is useful when several nodes overlap or the graph contains many similarly labelled accounts. Find the record by its available name or reference, select it and inspect the associated details before expanding its neighbourhood. View controls improve readability but do not change the source records. When removing a node from the canvas, remember that structural analysis can still operate over the loaded graph, including records hidden from the current view.'),
'07-network-types': ('Layout as an analytical choice', 'The same relationships can appear very different when organised by type, community or direction of transfer. Choose the layout to make the relevant question easier to examine, then confirm the facts in the inspector. A layout is a presentation method rather than additional evidence. Comparing two views can help identify which apparent pattern is a feature of the records and which is produced by their arrangement on the canvas.'),
'07-network-analysis': ('Reading a structural result', 'A record with high betweenness appears on routes between other loaded records, while an articulation point has a specific effect on connectivity if removed. These are different structural properties and should be described accurately. Inspect the selected node or edge to understand whether it represents a person, account, device or another object. The analytical result can guide follow-up, but the case explanation must relate it to the actual type, date and source of each relationship.'),
'07-network-community': ('Checking the underlying links', 'A community or dense core identifies a pattern in connectivity. Examine the kinds of links within that group and the records connecting it to other groups before assigning a substantive meaning. A shared service can connect many otherwise unrelated records. Similarly, a directed cycle that combines different relationship types is not a transaction sequence. The inspector and source records provide the evidence needed to distinguish those cases.'),
'07-network-money': ('Comparing financial routes', 'Read the layout from its source side toward the available destinations, then inspect the lanes relevant to the enquiry. A wider lane indicates a larger represented amount; it does not by itself explain why the transfer occurred. Consider whether the view aggregates several transactions and check their dates in the underlying records. If a route appears incomplete, inspect the selected scope and available accounts before assuming that the displayed endpoint is the ultimate recipient.'),
'07-network-inspector': ('Choosing a path method', 'The method determines the question answered. A shortest path identifies a small number of connecting links, while directed and money-only options constrain which relationships can be followed. Alternative routes can expose different chains between the endpoints. Inspect each component before using the result in a report, particularly when the chain includes inferred associations. The available paths are drawn from the loaded graph and should not be described as an exhaustive account of all possible relationships outside it.'),
'08-dossier-overview': ('Establishing the selected identity', 'Check the entity’s identifying details and source photograph before moving to an analytical tab. Similar names, shared addresses and associated family records require contextual reading. The overview’s case assessment describes the entity’s relevance within this investigation, while the other tabs provide the facts needed to examine it. Use the source view where an identifier or association requires explanation.'),
'08-dossier-overview-2': ('Interpreting a suggested next step', 'The assessment brings relevant facts and available actions into the same view. It helps the officer choose what to examine next, but it does not require a particular investigative conclusion. Open the cited financial activity, relationship or pattern and assess its source. Creating a lead or watch should follow a reasoned decision about the enquiry, rather than the presence of an action button beside the assessment.'),
'08-dossier-unmask': ('Protected information in context', 'A reveal request should relate to the work being performed in the active case. The recorded reason allows later review of why the additional detail was sought. Permission and storage limits remain separate: a user may be eligible for an action while the complete original value was never retained. A masked identifier should therefore be read as a protected or limited representation, not silently treated as a missing field or a value that can always be reconstructed.'),
'08-dossier-record': ('Distinguishing records from conclusions', 'A person may appear in a source record in different roles and at different stages of a proceeding. Read the role, status and reference together rather than treating every entry as the same kind of finding. Pattern results are additional analytical material and require their own examination. When preparing a report, identify which source supports the statement and whether it describes an allegation, an observation, an association or an established recorded outcome.'),
'08-dossier-financials': ('From summary to transaction', 'Use the monthly display to identify the period requiring examination, then narrow the transaction rows by account or amount. Check direction, narration and counterparty before describing a movement of funds. Transfers touching flagged accounts are prompts for review, not independent conclusions about their purpose. Where further movement is relevant, use the onward trace and retain the transaction reference so that the narrative can be checked against the source rather than only against an aggregate chart.'),
'08-dossier-network': ('Reading entity-centred relationships', 'The dossier network starts from the selected entity and provides a relationship context for the other tabs. Inspect the edge type before interpreting a connection to another person or object. Shared assets, communication identifiers and direct financial interactions are different kinds of association. Where a relationship appears relevant, use the inspector or linked dossier to examine its source and timing before carrying it into a line of enquiry.'),
'08-dossier-comms': ('Understanding the metadata', 'A contact total summarises records associated with identifiers. Review first and last interaction times and the underlying subscriber context before describing the relationship between people. An hourly distribution can identify a period worth examining, but it does not establish a routine beyond the source coverage. Compare relevant events with the timeline and other case material, and preserve the distinction between a recorded device interaction and evidence about the participants or content of a conversation.'),
'08-dossier-assets': ('Assessing ownership and movement', 'A booking reference, a registration and an asset record answer different questions. Inspect the source to determine whether it records a reservation, an observed journey, a registered owner or another relationship. Family-associated assets should retain the person to whom the source attributes them. The combined view can identify matters requiring further examination, but any conclusion about use, control or disproportionate ownership requires the relevant dates, values and supporting records.'),
'08-dossier-timeline': ('Constructing an evidence-based sequence', 'Start with the period relevant to the case and open the records behind significant events. Several source types may use dates with different meanings, such as an event date, filing date or transaction timestamp. Consider these differences when linking entries into a sequence. The newest-first view and paging support navigation through available material; they do not imply that every event in the person’s activity has been captured or that nearby entries are causally related.'),
'08-dossier-sources': ('Resolving a question about a fact', 'When a consolidated value is disputed, identify the source row and the context in which it arrived. Compare any competing values rather than assuming the displayed field is the only available version. Matching information helps explain why a row was associated with the entity, while the original source provides its own wording and identifiers. This distinction is useful when deciding whether the issue concerns a source error, an identity association or the interpretation of an otherwise accurate record.'),
'09-money': ('Interpreting the endpoint', 'An outward path shows the available movement from the selected starting point under the chosen limits. Examine whether an endpoint represents a foreign destination, a cash withdrawal, another recorded party or simply the end of available data. Changing the hop or amount threshold can alter the displayed route. Before reporting a destination, check the relevant account and transaction references and distinguish the observed transfer from any inference about subsequent use or control of the funds.'),
'09-money-in': ('Examining available origins', 'An inward trace asks which recorded transfers lead to the selected endpoint. Review the contributing accounts and the level of aggregation before describing an originator. A party can represent several accounts, while an account-level view keeps those instruments separate. The earliest displayed point is bounded by the available records and query limits; it should not automatically be treated as the original economic source of the funds.'),
'09-money-accounts': ('Why account detail matters', 'Keeping accounts separate helps the officer identify which instrument received or sent a particular transfer. Party aggregation can conceal movement between accounts associated with the same holder, while account detail makes that distinction available for examination. Compare ownership or control references with the transaction path before assigning responsibility. The choice of presentation changes how the route is read, not the recorded evidence behind the transfers.'),
'10-connect-start': ('Formulating the relationship question', 'Use one endpoint when examining the selected record’s available associates. Add the second when the enquiry concerns a particular pair of records. Check identifiers and context in the selection results to avoid comparing the wrong objects. The view can then expose the available route or associations between those endpoints. The investigative explanation should state which relationship is being examined and why it is relevant, rather than treating any possible path as equally significant.'),
'10-connect': ('Reading a chain of associations', 'Consider each link as a separate statement supported by a particular record. A path through a shared company, phone or address has a different meaning from a direct transaction. Check whether the links relate to the period under examination and distinguish inferred associations from recorded interactions. The chain can identify useful source material or further questions, but its existence alone does not establish that the endpoints acted together or knew of one another’s activity.'),
'11-globe': ('Interpreting the selected scope', 'A broad map can reveal geographic distribution, but the available layers represent different types of information. Select an item to determine whether its position comes from an address, observation, travel record or other source. Relationship lines may describe connections rather than physical movement along the drawn route. Use the map to locate relevant material, then examine the associated dossier or trace before including a geographic interpretation in the case narrative.'),
'11-globe-zoom': ('Moving from overview to detail', 'Zoom and clustering change how the same permitted records are presented. A cluster indicates several mapped items within the view, not a single combined event. Open the relevant item and inspect its source context before connecting it to the nearby observations. The local view can help choose an area for a more explicit radius-and-time enquiry, where the search parameters make the geographic question clearer.'),
'11-area-panel': ('Defining a reproducible area enquiry', 'The selected centre, radius and time window determine which observations can match the enquiry. Record a reason that relates those parameters to the investigation. A narrower window can answer a particular event question, while a broader one may include unrelated activity. After running the search, review the source coverage alongside the results so that the absence of an observation is not interpreted beyond what the available sources can establish.'),
'11-area-results': ('Comparing observations', 'Read each result’s type, first and last recorded times and available location references. The relationship from a vehicle or phone to a person depends on the owner or subscriber records and may require further examination. Registered residents provide geographic context but are not themselves observations during the selected interval. Follow a relevant result into the dossier or network, preserving the search parameters and source basis when using the material in a report.'),
'11-area-results-2': ('Coverage and absence', 'A result set is only as informative as the sources and period covered by the enquiry. Check any indication that no applicable sensor or observation source is available. An empty result from an uncovered area cannot establish that no relevant activity occurred. Likewise, an observed identifier requires assessment of its relationship to a person. These distinctions should accompany any case narrative that relies on the area search.'),
'12-explore': ('From a summary to its records', 'An aggregate can identify a district, period or incident category requiring closer examination. Selecting that summary applies a narrower context to the underlying records. Inspect those records before interpreting the distribution, especially where source completeness or reporting dates differ. The result can support a more focused query or case selection, while the source references provide the basis for explaining what the aggregate actually represents.'),
'12-explore-results': ('Developing the enquiry', 'Use the result’s properties and relationships to determine whether it is the object required by the case. When several records have similar names, compare identifiers and provenance rather than relying on order in the list. A saved search retains the question for later review, while adding a record to a case connects the selected material to current work. These actions serve different purposes and should follow the officer’s assessment of the record’s relevance.'),
'13-studio-columns': ('Inspecting an incoming extract', 'Review the values represented by each column rather than assuming that the header accurately describes them. Missing entries, inconsistent formats and unexpected values can affect mapping and identity matching later in the workflow. The profile provides a basis for choosing the cleaning steps and record interpretation. Before proceeding, confirm that the selected source and case context correspond to the extract being prepared, so that subsequent proposed changes remain connected to the correct origin.'),
'13-studio-cleanup': ('Reviewing transformations', 'An effective cleaning recipe addresses a specific representation problem while preserving the meaning of the source. Inspect changed cells and quarantined rows to understand what was accepted, transformed or excluded from the proposal. Duplicate treatment should be assessed against the extract’s identifiers and row structure. Reset and preview controls allow the officer to compare the effect of the recipe before carrying its results into matching and publication. The original source remains the reference for that assessment.'),
'13-studio-fields': ('Representing the source accurately', 'Mapping determines the meaning of a value in the shared record model. Review whether a column describes an object’s property or an identifier used to connect it with another object. A join can add a relationship, so its basis should be checked as carefully as the field itself. Inspect unmapped fields and blocked classes before proceeding. The later changes view allows the reviewer to see the operations produced by these choices rather than approving an unexplained transformation.'),
'13-studio-matching': ('Assessing the proposed identity', 'An exact agreement in a specific identifier differs from a probable association based on less complete information. Read the matching explanation and compare any conflicting fields before deciding whether the proposal is suitable for publication. The category filters separate rows requiring different kinds of attention. When a check creates a lens, clear it after examining that subset so that the reviewer can return to the full extract and avoid overlooking other proposed operations.'),
'13-studio-checks': ('Reviewing a flagged condition', 'Open the supporting rows to determine whether the condition reflects a meaningful issue, a source convention or an ordinary relationship. The check’s possible explanation is a prompt for investigation, not an automatic resolution. Use Checked or Dismiss to record the disposition of the individual item. Review the proposed links and places where they help clarify the effect of publication, and consider conflicts separately from new information that simply fills an existing gap.'),
'13-studio-changes': ('Assessing publication scope', 'The changes view makes the effect of the intake concrete. Review new objects separately from additions to existing ones, and distinguish corroboration from disagreement. A conflict requires interpretation of the source and its date; it is not permission to replace an existing value silently. The approving officer should be able to relate the proposed operations to the order, source rows and explanatory note before deciding whether publication is appropriate.'),
'13-studio-tagging': ('Reading a tagged reference', 'A highlighted span identifies a piece of text that can be compared with the record model. Read its surrounding sentence or entry before assigning significance: a number can be mentioned without describing an ownership relationship. The same-page identifier requirement for name association provides an additional basis for a proposed link. The officer should still examine the linked record and source context before relying on the tag in a case narrative or publication decision.'),
'13-studio-diary': ('Preserving document context', 'The source text helps the reviewer assess how an identifier is being used in the document. A name and an account reference may appear together, but the wording can affect what that association means. Inspect the highlighted span and linked record together before treating the tag as evidence of ownership or involvement. The tagging workflow supplies traceable references for review; it does not convert every textual co-occurrence into an established relationship.'),
'13-studio-proposed': ('What the reviewer receives', 'Submission fixes the proposed operations for a separate decision. The preparing officer should check that the order and note explain the extract and the intended publication. The proposal state then communicates whether the intake is awaiting review or has received a decision. A later reviewer can assess the same submitted operations, rather than a preparation session that continues to change while approval is being considered.'),
'13-studio-review': ('Reasoning about the decision', 'The decision should address the actual operations and their supporting context. Approval publishes those changes; rejection records why the submitted proposal should not be applied. If published operations later require reversal, use the recorded revert workflow and explain the reason. These are distinct actions with different effects, so the team should inspect the current proposal state before deciding which operation is appropriate.'),
'13-studio-published': ('Following publication', 'Review the resulting changes in the case context, particularly where the intake adds relationships or records that affect an existing line of enquiry. A subsequent detector run considers the newly available material, but any new finding still requires examination. If a source or mapping error is discovered, assess the affected publication and use the recorded correction or revert process. This preserves a visible distinction between the original submission, the approval and any later remedial action.'),
'14-review': ('Comparing evidence of identity', 'Read the fields together rather than counting agreements in isolation. A common name can be shared by unrelated people, while a conflicting date or identifier may be more informative. Consider whether the original sources refer to the same period and whether a missing field represents unknown information rather than disagreement. The recorded reason should explain the evidence relied upon so that another reviewer can understand the association or its rejection without repeating the whole comparison.'),
'14-review-pair': ('Writing an informative reason', 'Describe the fields that influenced the decision and how their sources support the interpretation. A reason such as matching identity references or conflicting birth details is more useful than a bare statement of confidence. If the evidence is insufficient, further source examination may be required before deciding. The review action should remain distinguishable from the original system proposal so that the basis of the resulting record treatment is clear.'),
'15-faces-empty': ('Preparing a suitable probe', 'Confirm that the selected image is the intended frame and that its source context is correctly recorded before adding it as evidence. The workflow then connects later face selection to that registered still. Where detection does not identify a usable face, select a clearer or closer image rather than assuming that the absence of a detection means the person is absent from the source material.'),
'15-faces-found': ('Defining the comparison', 'The selected face determines which part of the still is used for the search. Check the highlighted subject carefully when several people are present, then record a reason related to the case enquiry. The comparison is limited to photographs available in the permitted context. Its output should be assessed as candidate material for a subsequent review, with the source still retained as the reference for what was searched.'),
'15-faces-candidates': ('Reviewing a candidate', 'Compare the candidate photograph with the selected face while considering the source and image conditions. Similarity provides a ranking for examination, not independent proof of identity. Open the available contextual record and identify other material that supports or contradicts the association. The separate reviewer should record the reasoning behind confirmation or rejection so that the resulting decision can be understood beyond the numerical similarity shown on the screen.'),
'15-faces-confirm': ('Meaning of the recorded decision', 'Confirmation records that the second officer has reviewed the proposed association and accepted it for the workflow with a reason. Rejection records the contrary disposition. Neither result should erase the distinction between the comparison generated by the system and the evidence considered by the reviewer. The retained reason explains why the human decision was reached and helps later examination of the case record.'),
'16-cop': ('Reading alert currency and geography', 'An alert should be interpreted according to its effective period and source status. Mapping by an explicit geometry differs from placement derived from a district name or gauge, so inspect the mapping basis before drawing a precise geographic conclusion. Unmapped entries remain relevant and should be reviewed through the list. The view depends on ingested material; a visually current map does not itself establish that the external feed has just been refreshed.'),
'16-cop-flood': ('Planning from the available information', 'Review the alert’s timing and severity together with the affected districts. Gauge levels and hospital references provide additional context, but their presence does not establish available capacity or completed response arrangements. Use the dispatch form to record the allocation being made in the application and verify the resulting log entry. Communication with responders and confirmation of physical delivery remain part of the institution’s operational process outside the displayed record.'),
'16-cop-dispatch': ('Checking the allocation entry', 'Before recording the dispatch, confirm that the destination district corresponds to the intended response area and that the quantity is entered in the relevant resource context. The log supports coordination by preserving the allocation entered by the operator. It should be reviewed alongside the alert’s validity and the institution’s external response arrangements, rather than treated as an automatic confirmation that supplies or personnel have arrived.'),
'17-oversight': ('Interpreting an audit event', 'An event describes the action recorded at a particular time, including its actor, case, purpose and outcome. Read these fields together when assessing whether access was consistent with the stated work. A refused request can be as relevant to the enquiry as an allowed one. Integrity checks address the recorded chain, while assessment of the action’s appropriateness requires its institutional context. The auditor’s separate role allows this examination without using the investigator’s personal-record workspace.'),
'17-oversight-filtered': ('A targeted oversight question', 'Filtering by record identifies activity associated with that reference; filtering by user examines the actor’s recorded actions. Using both narrows the question further. Review the time, purpose and outcome of each event rather than assuming that all returned entries represent successful access. If the review requires an integrity reference outside the application, the sealed root must be independently retained or published through the institution’s operational process.'),
'17-transparency': ('Separating summaries from lifecycle actions', 'Aggregate transparency figures communicate activity at a summary level. The retention panel addresses the handling of cases according to their eligibility and hold state. A case listed as due has a different status from one whose purge has completed. Review the recorded manifests to understand actions actually performed, and inspect hold reasons where material remains retained. These views answer related governance questions but should not be treated as interchangeable evidence.'),
'18-admin-library': ('Using method documentation', 'Before interpreting a detector output, establish which records it expects and which conditions it is designed to identify. Review the stated ordinary explanations and blind spots alongside the method. An unexpected result may arise from source quality, incomplete coverage or an implementation issue, and those possibilities require different responses. The documentation gives the reviewer a basis for asking the right question; it does not remove the need to inspect the actual evidence behind a finding.'),
'18-admin-quality': ('Coordinating a correction', 'Use the affected record and source identifiers to locate the original material and describe the issue precisely to its owner. Determine whether the concern relates to a value, mapping or missing context before proposing a correction. Any updated extract can then be considered through the applicable preparation and publication workflow. This maintains the distinction between identifying a source problem and applying a change to shared case information.'),
}
